{
 "id": "2026-11-17-deep-sea-nodule-animals",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "Two hundred forty-five animals inside a single deep-sea rock. Why do miners care?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "A study published in Scientific Reports looked inside two hundred forty-four metal-rich nodules from the Clarion-Clipperton Zone in the central Pacific. Dutch and German researchers counted four thousand one hundred thirty-nine animals, about seventeen per rock."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "Most are nematode worms and tiny crustaceans called copepods, smaller than a millimeter, sheltering in cracks inside the rock. The team found fewer animals than in the surrounding mud, but distinct communities. Bigger nodules held more animals."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "The nodules hold nickel, cobalt and copper, metals used in batteries. They grow only a few millimeters per million years, the researchers say, so mining would destroy this habitat for good. In the U.S., NOAA is reviewing a mining application from The Metals Company."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will the U.S. approve deep-sea nodule mining by spring twenty twenty-seven? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
