 "upload": {
  "title": "Thousands of Tiny Animals Live Inside Deep-Sea Rocks #shorts",
  "description": "A study published in Scientific Reports on November 11 found 4,139 tiny animals, mostly nematode worms and copepods, living in the cracks of 244 polymetallic nodules from the Clarion-Clipperton Zone in the central Pacific, with up to 245 in a single nodule. The NIOZ and MARUM researchers say these communities differ from those in the surrounding mud, and that mining the slow-growing, metal-rich nodules would destroy this habitat permanently. NOAA is reviewing The Metals Company's U.S. application for commercial nodule recovery.\n\nSources: Scientific Reports paper (Polymetallic nodules provide a distinct habitat for abyssal meiofauna), MARUM release via EurekAlert, NIOZ release, Phys.org, ScienceDaily, ScienceAlert, The Debrief, Ocean News, Oceanographic, NOAA.\n\n#science #deepsea #ocean #deepseamining #biodiversity",
